<h2>Automating document collection without the chaos</h2>

<p>One of the biggest pain points in onboarding is collecting documents and information from new clients. You send an email asking for their logo. They reply with a low-resolution screenshot. You ask for their social media logins. They text you a password with no username. You request a signed agreement. They print it, sign it with a pen, photograph it at an angle, and email the blurry image back.</p>

<p>The fix is to centralise everything. Build a single intake form or onboarding portal where clients can upload files, fill in details, and sign documents in one place. Most modern CRM and <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">automation platforms</a> let you create these forms without writing a line of code.</p>

<p>Structure your form with clear labels and instructions. Instead of "Upload your logo," try "Upload your logo in PNG or SVG format, minimum 1000px wide. If you are not sure where to find this, check with your web designer or try right-clicking your website logo and saving the image." The more specific you are, the fewer back-and-forth messages you will need.</p>

<p>Set up automatic reminders for incomplete forms. If a client has not finished their intake form within 48 hours, a gentle nudge goes out. After five days, a slightly more direct reminder. This is not pestering. It is helping them clear a task off their plate so you can start delivering value sooner.</p>

<h2>Setting expectations early (and saving yourself headaches later)</h2>

<p>Most client frustrations come from mismatched expectations, not poor service. The client expected weekly updates and you planned to check in monthly. They thought the project would take two weeks and you quoted four. They assumed revisions were unlimited and you had a cap of three.</p>

<p>Your onboarding workflow is the perfect place to address all of this. Include a clear "how we work" section in your welcome materials. Cover response times, communication channels, project timelines, revision policies, and anything else that could become a source of friction later.</p>

<p>Be specific. "We respond to emails within one business day. If something is urgent, text us at this number. We send progress updates every Friday. Your project timeline is [X weeks] from the date we receive all materials." These details feel small, but they prevent the conversations that start with "I have been waiting a week and have not heard anything from you."</p>

<p>When expectations are set clearly from the start, clients feel informed and confident. When they are left vague, clients fill in the gaps with their own assumptions, and those assumptions almost never match reality.</p>

