<h2>Building your workflow step by step</h2>

<p>If you are starting from scratch, here is a simple framework to follow. You do not need to build everything at once. Start with the basics and refine over time.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Map the journey.</strong> Write down every step that happens between a client saying yes and the work being fully underway. Include every email, every form, every call, every document. Get it all on paper (or a whiteboard, or a notes app).</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Identify the automatable parts.</strong> Look at your list and circle everything that does not require your personal involvement. Welcome emails, intake forms, reminders, progress updates, and feedback requests can all be automated. The kickoff call and the actual service delivery need you.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Write the messages.</strong> Draft every <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email</a> and SMS in your onboarding sequence. Keep them friendly, clear, and concise. Read them out loud to check if they sound like a person, not a corporation.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Build it in your platform.</strong> Set up the triggers (client signs up, form submitted, date reached) and the actions (send email, wait two days, send reminder). Connect your intake forms and document collection to the same system so everything lives in one place.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5: Test it yourself.</strong> Run through the entire sequence as if you were a new client. Sign up, receive the welcome email, fill out the form, get the reminders. Make sure everything fires correctly and the experience feels smooth.</p>

<p><strong>Step 6: Gather data and improve.</strong> After running your onboarding workflow for a month, review what is working. Are clients completing the intake form quickly? Are they responding to check-ins? Use that data to tweak the timing, the messaging, or the sequence itself. Your <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">follow-up system</a> can feed into your onboarding to keep the whole process tight.</p>

<blockquote><p>A great onboarding workflow is never finished. It is a living system that gets better every time you learn something new about what your clients need.</p></blockquote>

